---
/*
  Synopsis: the welcome text on the home page, from Erin's draft wording
  (DRAFT WORDING, "Front page", 8 November 2026), with the popcorn beside
  the heading and the theatre picture alongside on wide screens.

  The trouper/trooper passage is paraphrased from Merriam-Webster, not a
  direct quote, so it has no quotation marks and is credited as paraphrased.

  Mustard text on maroon: approved pair 8.50:1.
  The popcorn and theatre are decoration (alt="").

  Props: none.
*/
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import popcorn from '../assets/illustrations/popcorn.png';
import theatre from '../assets/illustrations/theatre-entry.jpg';
---

<section class="synopsis container" aria-labelledby="synopsis-heading">
  <div class="synopsis__text">
    <div class="synopsis__top">
      <h2 id="synopsis-heading" class="synopsis__heading">Synopsis</h2>
      <Image class="synopsis__popcorn" src={popcorn} alt="" widths={[200, 400]} sizes="10rem" />
    </div>

    <p>
      It’s Cancer website is your backstage pass to living through and after a cancer diagnosis –
      filled with support, advice, tips and tricks, we hope to help ease the journey.
    </p>
    <p>
      As cancer patients we are performing different scripts for different crowds, but all with the
      same intent – getting through it the best we can.
    </p>

    <div class="synopsis__paraphrase">
      <p>
        The words, trouper and trooper can both be used to describe someone going through hardship or
        difficulty. Trooper originates from the designation given to soldiers and police officers, who
        are no strangers to difficult conditions in the line of duty. Whereas trouper originates from
        one who is part of a theatre troupe and thus realizes the show must go on.
      </p>
      <p class="synopsis__source">– Merriam-Webster Dictionary (paraphrased)</p>
    </div>

    <p>
      I believe as we don’t sign up for this, nor do we train for the battle – we reflect those who
      improvise, perform different emotions for different scenes and go through the steps that is
      everyday life. We are troupers and the show must go on.
    </p>
    <p>So, welcome to the troupe – we bloody wish you weren’t here, but pleased you found us.</p>
    <p class="synopsis__signoff">Chookas!</p>
  </div>

  <Image
    class="synopsis__theatre"
    src={theatre}
    alt=""
    widths={[400, 800, 1200]}
    sizes="(min-width: 64rem) 30vw, 0px"
  />
</section>

<style>
  .synopsis {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-2xl);
    padding-block: var(--space-xl) var(--space-3xl);
    color: var(--colour-mustard);
  }

  .synopsis__top {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--space-l);
    margin-block-end: var(--space-l);
  }

  .synopsis__heading {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-script);
    font-weight: 400;
    font-size: clamp(3rem, 9vw, 5rem);
    line-height: 1.1;
    /* No red outline (as in the mock-up): against thin script letters it
       puts mustard on red, a banned pair (2.84:1) */
  }

  .synopsis__popcorn {
    width: clamp(5rem, 15vw, 9rem);
  }

  .synopsis__paraphrase {
    margin-block-end: var(--space-m);
    padding-inline-start: var(--space-l);
    border-inline-start: 4px solid var(--colour-mustard);
  }

  .synopsis__source {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .synopsis__signoff {
    font-family: var(--font-script);
    font-size: var(--text-h2);
  }

  /* The theatre picture only shows when there's room beside the text */
  .synopsis__theatre {
    display: none;
  }

  @media (min-width: 64rem) {
    .synopsis {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 70ch) minmax(0, 1fr);
      align-items: start;
    }

    .synopsis__theatre {
      display: block;
      width: 100%;
      border-radius: var(--radius);
    }
  }
</style>
